 "rulebook": "World Athletics Competition and Technical Rules 2024: TR 32.5-32.8 (the circle), TR 32.12 (the sector), TR 32.13, 32.14, 32.16, 32.17 and 32.20 (throwing events: the start, failures, the landing, leaving the circle, the measurement), TR 33.1 (the put from the shoulder), TR 33.2 (the stop board), TR 33.4-33.5 (the men's shot)",
 "deviations": [
  "TR 33.1 (\"the shot shall touch or be in close proximity to the neck or the chin\"; the hand that holds it): the H1-2's hand is a flat rubber palm pad without fingers and nothing grips: the shot rests on the open right palm, which faces up and in towards the neck, and leans against the neck (the trunk's hull: the H1-2's trunk and head are one hull), held only by the palm's support, the neck and friction; it rolls or slides off when the palm does not carry it. The stance is given at the start (the shot's centre within 0.22 m of the line from the base of the neck to the chin). From then to the release the rule is judged on the shot's centre in the trunk's frame: \"the hand shall not be dropped below this position\" as not lower than 0.0 m below the line of the two shoulder joints, \"not behind the line of the shoulders\" as not more than 0.0 m behind that line, and \"put\" (not thrown or slung) as: at the release (the right hand's last contact) the shot moves away from the putting shoulder's joint with at least 0.5 of its speed relative to that joint.",
  "The hand: the palm is modelled as the soft part of a hand: it absorbs the shot (a ball thrown at a still palm keeps 0.12 of its speed; friction 0.7), the contact of the two-palm basketball families (dunk-realgrasp); the shot's contact with the rest of the robot, the ground and the stop board is its own (rebound 0.10).",
  "TR 32.5-32.6: the ground outside the circle is level with the rim's top for 1 m and then falls to the field's level over 1 m (the circle's inside is the field's level, 0.02 m below the rim, instead of the field being level with the rim).",
  "TR 32.20: the mark is the point where the shot first touches the ground, not the nearest edge of its dent.",
  "TR 32.15 (interrupting a trial, laying the implement down): not modelled; the first release is the put, and nothing of the robot may touch the shot again.",
  "TR 32.14 names no fall: here a fall ends the trial as a failure, as in every task of this benchmark; the trial ends when the robot has left the circle correctly and stands.",
  "TR 25.17 (one minute for a trial): the whole trial, leaving the circle included, has 60 s.",
  "Flight: the shot's drag in still air (MuJoCo's ellipsoid fluid model, a quasi-steady model), no wind.",
  "The qualifying mark of 6.0 m is this benchmark's (a school standard for the 7.26 kg shot); the rule book only measures."
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Put the shot that rests on your open right palm at your neck, from the shoulder with that hand alone, so that it lands inside the sector at least 6.0 m from the circle, then leave the circle from its rear half and stand.",
  "rules": "The men's shot (7.26 kg, 0.12 m across) starts resting on your open right palm beside your neck, leaning against the neck: you have taken your stance. Your hands have no fingers and no grip (the grip columns do nothing; nothing is grippable): only the palm's support, your neck and friction hold the shot, and it rolls or slides off when the palm does not carry it. The circle is 2.135 m across inside its rim; the ground outside is 0.02 m higher than its inside. At the front, a stop board 1.21 m long, 0.112 m wide and 0.1 m high follows the rim. The sector opens from the circle's centre, 34.92 degrees wide, symmetric about the direction you face at the start. Holding: the shot touches your right hand (the palm pad and the wrist); breaks of that contact shorter than 0.03 s are part of the hold. The release is the hand's last contact before a break of at least 0.03 s; at that moment of the break's end the shot must touch no part of you (a shot that leaves the hand but still rests on your trunk is a failure). The put (TR 33.1), judged on the shot's centre relative to your trunk (the line through your two shoulder joints, your trunk's up and forward directions) from the start to the release: the centre never lower than 0.0 m below the line of the shoulders and never more than 0.0 m behind it; nothing but your right hand and your trunk touches the shot; and at the release the shot must be moving away from your right shoulder joint with at least 0.5 of its speed relative to that joint (a push from the shoulder, not a throw or a sling). There is one release: after it nothing of you may touch the shot. The shot's first touch of the ground must be inside the sector lines (on a line is out); the put is measured from that point to the inside of the rim along the line through the circle's centre. You succeed when a valid put measures at least 6.0 m and you have then left the circle correctly and stood for 1.0 s. Leaving correctly: after the shot has landed, your first touch of the rim's top or of the ground outside the circle is behind the line through the circle's centre across the putting direction (the rear half). Standing: only your feet on the ground, the torso within about 20 degrees of upright, the pelvis slower than 0.35 m/s. You fail if any part of you touches the rim's top (its top inside edge included), the ground outside the circle or any part of the stop board other than its inner side (its top edge is not its inner side) before the shot has landed, or in the front half before you have left correctly; if the shot drops below or goes behind the line of the shoulders (falling off the palm included), is touched by anything but the right hand and the trunk, leaves the hand onto the trunk, or is not pushed at the release; if it lands outside the sector or short of 6.0 m; if any part of you touches it after the release; if you fall (your pelvis lower than 0.5 m above the circle's floor, or any part of you other than your feet on the ground); or after 60 s. Where you touch the ground is judged on the points of contact; a contact within 0.003 m of the rim's top, or that far outside the stop board's inner side, counts as on the top. The neck line of the stance runs from 0.08 m above the middle of the shoulder line to the chin, 0.2 m above and 0.09 m in front of it; at the start the shot's centre is within 0.22 m of it.",
  "venue": "You stand in the rear half of the circle facing the sector (+x), your pelvis 0.45 m behind the circle's centre; the stop board is at the front of the circle, 1.5 m ahead of you. Your right arm is folded up in front of the right side of your chest, the open palm facing up, in towards the neck and a little back, the shot resting on it against the right side of the neck, just above and in front of the right shoulder. The start command is not where the arm stands (the joint table's start targets differ from its angles: the difference carries the shot's weight on the soft arm servos); held as it is, the shot stays and the robot stands (its pelvis drifts about 2 cm in the minute). Still air.",
  "difficulty": "The shot weighs 7.26 kg and rests on a flat palm without fingers: every move of the trunk or the arm that tilts the palm or accelerates the hand sideways rolls it off, and the H1-2's arm motors (40 N m at the shoulder, 18 N m at the elbow, soft servos) only just carry it, so the legs and the trunk have to drive the put while the palm stays under and behind the shot, inside a circle two steps wide. 6.0 m needs a release near 7 m/s at about 40 degrees."
 },
